# Auth

This app ships with a generated auth package under `internal/auth`.

The generated setup gives you:

- local username or email plus password sign-in
- cookie-based browser auth with `HttpOnly` cookies
- short-lived access JWTs plus server-authoritative refresh sessions
- current-user, logout, logout-all, session listing, and per-session revoke
- password change
- password reset
- email verification
- login rate limiting and temporary account lockout
- auth-owned cleanup of stale auth rows
{{- if .Components.OAuth }}
- optional OAuth provider sign-in and account linking
{{- end }}

## Component Model

Generated auth is a top-level framework component.

- `Auth` provides the baseline account and session system.
- `Mail` is used by auth delivery flows such as password reset and email verification.
{{- if .Components.OAuth }}
- `OAuth` is layered on top of baseline auth for provider sign-in and linking.
{{- end }}

Generated component rules:

- `Auth` requires `WebAPI` and a database.
- `Auth` also implies `Mail`.
{{- if .Components.OAuth }}
- `OAuth` requires `Auth` and a database.
{{- end }}

## Generated HTTP API

Baseline auth routes:

- `POST /api/v1/auth/login`
- `POST /api/v1/auth/logout`
- `POST /api/v1/auth/logout-all`
- `POST /api/v1/auth/refresh`
- `GET /api/v1/auth/me`
- `GET /api/v1/auth/sessions`
- `POST /api/v1/auth/sessions/:id/revoke`
- `POST /api/v1/auth/change-password`
- `POST /api/v1/auth/password-reset/request`
- `POST /api/v1/auth/password-reset/confirm`
- `POST /api/v1/auth/email-verification/request`
- `POST /api/v1/auth/email-verification/confirm`
{{- if .Components.OAuth }}

OAuth routes when the `OAuth` component is enabled:

- `GET /api/v1/auth/oauth/:provider/start`
- `GET /api/v1/auth/oauth/:provider/callback`
- `POST /api/v1/auth/oauth/:provider/callback`
- `GET /api/v1/auth/oauth/:provider/link/start`
{{- end }}

## Cookies And Session Model

Generated auth is server-authoritative.

- `auth_access` stores the short-lived access JWT.
- `auth_refresh` stores the opaque refresh-backed session token.
- refresh tokens are backed by persisted rows in `auth_sessions`.
- logout and revoke operate on persisted session state, not just cookie deletion.
- refresh rotation is mandatory.

This means JWTs are not treated as the only source of truth.

## Request Auth Behavior

Generated auth middleware follows this flow:

1. `RequireAuth()` first tries the access cookie.
2. If the access token is missing or expired, it attempts refresh.
3. If refresh succeeds, cookies rotate and the request continues.
4. Otherwise the request returns `401`, and dedicated auth endpoints such as refresh or logout own cookie invalidation.

This keeps browser auth smooth while still preserving explicit server-side revocation.

## Configuration

Use these environment variables:

```env
API_JWT_SECRET_KEY=

AUTH_ACCESS_TTL_SECONDS=900
AUTH_REFRESH_TTL_SECONDS=2592000
AUTH_PASSWORD_RESET_TTL_SECONDS=3600
AUTH_EMAIL_VERIFICATION_TTL_SECONDS=86400

AUTH_COOKIE_DOMAIN=
AUTH_COOKIE_SECURE=false
AUTH_COOKIE_SAME_SITE=lax

AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=900
AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5
AUTH_ACCOUNT_LOCKOUT_SECONDS=900
AUTH_LAST_SEEN_WRITE_INTERVAL_SECONDS=300

AUTH_BOOTSTRAP_USERNAME=admin
AUTH_BOOTSTRAP_EMAIL=admin@example.com
AUTH_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD=admin

AUTH_FROM_EMAIL=no-reply@example.com
AUTH_FROM_NAME={{.ProjectName}}
```

Common auth env vars:

| Env var | Value type | Default |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `API_JWT_SECRET_KEY` | string | empty, required outside generated local defaults |
| `AUTH_ACCESS_TTL_SECONDS` | integer seconds | 900 |
| `AUTH_REFRESH_TTL_SECONDS` | integer seconds | `30 * 24 * 3600` |
| `AUTH_PASSWORD_RESET_TTL_SECONDS` | integer seconds | 3600 |
| `AUTH_EMAIL_VERIFICATION_TTL_SECONDS` | integer seconds | 86400 |
| `AUTH_COOKIE_DOMAIN` | string | empty |
| `AUTH_COOKIE_SECURE` | boolean | false |
| `AUTH_COOKIE_SAME_SITE` | string | lax |
| `AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS` | integer seconds | 900 |
| `AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_MAX_ATTEMPTS` | integer | 5 |
| `AUTH_ACCOUNT_LOCKOUT_SECONDS` | integer seconds | 900 |
| `AUTH_LAST_SEEN_WRITE_INTERVAL_SECONDS` | integer seconds | 300 |
| `AUTH_BOOTSTRAP_USERNAME` | string | admin |
| `AUTH_BOOTSTRAP_EMAIL` | string | admin@example.com |
| `AUTH_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD` | string | admin |
| `AUTH_FROM_EMAIL` | string | no-reply@example.com |
| `AUTH_FROM_NAME` | string | app name fallback |

{{- if .Components.OAuth }}
OAuth env vars are included only when the `OAuth` component is enabled. Provider credentials are commented out by default in `.env`.
{{- end }}

## Data Model

Generated auth owns these core tables:

- `users`
- `auth_sessions`
- `auth_password_resets`
- `auth_email_verifications`
- `auth_login_attempts`
{{- if .Components.OAuth }}
- `auth_identities`
- `auth_oauth_states`
{{- end }}

Important `users` fields include:

- `username`
- `email`
- `display_name`
- `password_hash`
- `active`
- `failed_login_attempts`
- `email_verified_at`
- `last_login_at`
- `last_seen_at`
- `locked_until`

Important `auth_sessions` fields include:

- `id`
- `user_id`
- `refresh_token_hash`
- `expires_at`
- `revoked_at`
- `last_seen_at`
- `user_agent`
- `ip_address`

## Usage

Require auth on a route:

```go
web.NewRoute(http.MethodGet, "/auth/me", controller.Me, authService.RequireAuth())
```

Read the authenticated user inside a handler:

```go
user := auth.CurrentUser(c)
if user == nil {
	return c.JSON(http.StatusUnauthorized, map[string]any{"ok": false})
}
```

List current sessions inside a controller:

```go
sessions, err := authService.Sessions(r)
```

## Local Development

Generated auth stays easy to operate locally:

- local bootstrap user support exists for development flows
- explicit auth commands exist for user creation and password management
- reset and verification tokens can be returned directly in local workflows instead of requiring live mail delivery

Operator-oriented auth commands typically include:

- bootstrap local admin
- create user
- set password

## Notes

- Auth session persistence is the revocation anchor. Do not treat access JWTs as the only auth source of truth.
- Refresh rotation is intentional. Reuse of old refresh material should be treated as invalid.
- `Mail` should remain wired even if your app does not yet expose every auth flow in the UI.
- Keep this README aligned with `internal/auth` routes, env vars, and policy behavior whenever auth changes.
